        // Buggy: ok == false (burn reverted), totalSupply stays 100
        // So we assert that totalSupply is 99 on reference, but on buggy it's 100.
        // But setup is fixed to new C(0,0,0) — we cannot change that. So we need a different scenario.
        // Actually the problem says deploy with new C(0,0,0) — that gives totalSupply 0, balanceOf owner is 0, so burn always reverts even on reference.
        // So difference cannot be in totalSupply.
        // Let's use the frozenAccount of a non-frozen address that becomes frozen due to a call that only happens on reference.
        // The only way frozenAccount differs is if a successful call triggers another call that freezes something? No.
        // Rethink: the mutant changes onlyOwner to reject the owner. So any onlyOwner function called by owner will revert on buggy.
        // On reference, owner can call freezeAccount and burn.
        // If owner calls freezeAccount to freeze some address, that succeeds on reference but reverts on buggy.
        // Thus frozenAccount[target] will be false (default) on buggy but true on reference.
        // That's the divergence!
        // Let key be address(1). Owner calls freezeAccount(address(1), true).
        // Reference: succeeds, frozenAccount[1] = true.
        // Buggy: reverts (owner is rejected), frozenAccount[1] remains false.
        // So after the call, reference has frozenAccount[1] == true, buggy has false.
        // Assert that.
        c.freezeAccount(address(1), true);
